<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sheikh Zayed · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nahyan">Al Nahyan</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zayed bin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nahyan">Al Nahyan</a> was ruler of Abu Dhabi from 1966, when he replaced his brother Shakhbut, and used its new oil revenues for rapid development. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-bin-saeed-al-maktoum">Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum</a> of Dubai he led the talks among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a> that created the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> in 1971, and he served as its first president until his death in 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan.jpg" alt="Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan" width="225" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Archives of the United Arab Emirates, Ministry of Presidential Affairs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheikh_Zayed_with_King_Faisal,_1974_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zayed was born at Qasr Al Hosn, Abu Dhabi, in 1918, the youngest of four sons of Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> bin Khalifa Al Nahyan, who ruled Abu Dhabi from 1922 until his death in 1926. He was named after his grandfather, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-khalifa-al-nahyan">Zayed the Great</a>, who ruled from 1855 to 1909. At his birth Abu Dhabi was one of seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a> on the lower coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. After his father's death he moved to Al Ain, where no modern school existed on the coast. He received a basic instruction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and lived in the desert among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribesmen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1946 Zayed became governor of the Eastern Region of Abu Dhabi, based at Al Ain, and he assisted the oil parties exploring there. When a small Saudi force occupied the village of Hamasa in the Buraimi Oasis in 1952, he opposed the Saudi claims and gave evidence at the arbitration tribunal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in September 1955. Oil was discovered in 1958 and exports began in 1962. Members of the ruling family, frustrated that Sheikh Shakhbut would not spend the revenue on development, asked for British help, and on 6 August 1966 Shakhbut was deposed in a bloodless palace coup and replaced by Zayed.</p><p>In January 1968 the British announced that they would end their treaties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a> and withdraw. On 18 February 1968 Zayed and Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-bin-saeed-al-maktoum">Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum</a> of Dubai shook hands on the principle of founding a federation. After occasionally difficult negotiations with the other six rulers, the United Arab Emirates was formed in 1971, with Zayed as its president. He was re-elected in 1976, 1981, 1986 and 1991. In 1974 the Treaty of Jeddah settled the border dispute with Saudi Arabia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zayed used oil revenue to build hospitals, schools and universities, to which citizens had free access. In 1984 he financed the present dam at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Ma'rib</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. He died on 2 November 2004, aged 86, and was buried in the courtyard of the Grand Mosque in Abu Dhabi. His eldest son, Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa-bin-zayed-al-nahyan">Khalifa bin Zayed Al Nahyan</a>, succeeded him as ruler of Abu Dhabi and was ratified as president. Zayed is called Father of the Nation in the United Arab Emirates.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A month later <b>Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan</b>, ruler of Abu Dhabi since 1966, and Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum of Dubai agreed at a meeting in the desert between their two emirates to form a union and invited the other rulers to join them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1966 <b>Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan</b> replaced his brother Shakhbut and used the revenues for development.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-abu-dhabi">Sheikhdom of Abu Dhabi</a></li><li><blockquote>Rashid worked with <b>Zayed</b> of Abu Dhabi to form the United Arab Emirates, which Dubai joined at its foundation in December 1971.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-dubai">Sheikhdom of Dubai</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Abu Dhabi, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-arab-emirates">the United Arab Emirates</a>. Died in Abu Dhabi, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-arab-emirates">the United Arab Emirates</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.45,54.4&amp;year=1973" aria-label="Abu Dhabi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4564 5463h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4564 5463h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.45,54.4&amp;year=1973">What was here in 1973? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
